# CORS for the GitHub Pages sites that call MindRouter through this host from
# the browser: Promptulus (https://ai4ra.github.io), Data Crawler Carl and the
# NCURA workshop site (https://ui-insight.github.io), and Remembering Thamus
# (https://nate-layman.github.io). As of 2026-10-23 the host answers every
# origin with a fixed "Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://ai4ra.github.io",
# so a page on any other origin fails with "Load failed" on its first model call.
#
# Caddy's `header` directive takes one static value, so the way to allow several
# origins is a matcher on the Origin header and an echo of it back. In
# /etc/caddy/Caddyfile on nlayman-external, inside the site block, put the
# preflight handler ABOVE the existing `handle @api` block (handle blocks are
# evaluated in order, and the preflight must be answered before the proxy sees
# it), and replace the Promptulus-only header lines inside `handle @api` with the
# two `header @pages_origin` lines. Then: sudo systemctl reload caddy
#
# Check from a laptop; the header should echo whichever origin you send:
#   curl -sI -X OPTIONS -H "Origin: https://nate-layman.github.io" \
#     -H "Access-Control-Request-Method: POST" \
#     https://nlayman-external.insight.uidaho.edu/v1/chat/completions | grep -i access-control

@pages_origin {
    header Origin https://ai4ra.github.io
    header Origin https://ui-insight.github.io
    header Origin https://nate-layman.github.io
}
@pages_preflight {
    method OPTIONS
    path /v1/*
    header Origin https://ai4ra.github.io
    header Origin https://ui-insight.github.io
    header Origin https://nate-layman.github.io
}
handle @pages_preflight {
    header Access-Control-Allow-Origin "{http.request.header.Origin}"
    header Access-Control-Allow-Methods "GET, POST, OPTIONS"
    header Access-Control-Allow-Headers "Authorization, Content-Type"
    header Access-Control-Max-Age "600"
    header Vary "Origin"
    respond 204
}

# Inside the existing `handle @api { ... }` block, before `reverse_proxy`:
#
#     header @pages_origin >Access-Control-Allow-Origin "{http.request.header.Origin}"
#     header @pages_origin >Vary "Origin"
#
# The `>` defers the header until the proxied response is written, so the
# 401 for a bad key carries it too and the page can show MindRouter's message.
